  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n### Summary\n\nA missing check in the `HypoVault.executeWithdrawal` function allows a division by zero to occur if a withdrawal epoch is fulfilled with zero shares. A manager can trigger this condition (intentionally or accidentally) by calling `fulfillWithdrawals` with `sharesToFulfill = 0`. This creates a broken state for that epoch where all pending user withdrawals become permanently locked. Affected users cannot execute their withdrawals, as the transaction will always revert, nor can they cancel them, as the cancellation function only operates on the current epoch. This results in a permanent loss of access to user funds for that epoch unless a complex manual intervention is performed by the owner.\n\n### Root Cause\n\nThe vulnerability stems from a sequence of events across three functions:\n\n1.  **`fulfillWithdrawals`:** In `src/HypoVault.sol`, the manager can call this function with `sharesToFulfill = 0`. The function will execute successfully, advancing the `withdrawalEpoch` but setting `withdrawalEpochState` for the just-closed epoch with `sharesFulfilled = 0`.\n2.  **`executeWithdrawal`:** In `src/HypoVault.sol`, when a user tries to process a withdrawal from this broken epoch, the function attempts to calculate `assetsToWithdraw` using `Math.mulDiv(..., ..., _withdrawalEpochState.sharesFulfilled)`. Since `sharesFulfilled` is `0`, this call results in a division by zero, causing the transaction to revert.\n3.  **`cancelWithdrawal`:** In `src/HypoVault.sol`, this function only operates on the *current* `withdrawalEpoch`. It cannot be used to cancel a withdrawal request that is stuck in a *previous* epoch.\n\n#### Internal Pre-conditions\n\n1.  A user has a pending withdrawal request in an active withdrawal epoch, `E`.\n2.  The vault `manager` calls `fulfillWithdrawals` for epoch `E`, passing `sharesToFulfill = 0`.\n\n### External Pre-conditions\n\n1.  A user must have previously called `requestWithdrawal`.\n\n### Attack Path\n\nThis bug can be triggered by a malicious or mistaken manager:\n1.  A user, Alice, has shares in the vault and calls `requestWithdrawal(amount)` during epoch `E`.\n2.  The `manager` calls `fulfillWithdrawals(0, ...)`. This closes epoch `E`, setting `sharesFulfilled` for it to `0`, and advances the active withdrawal epoch to `E+1`.\n3.  Alice calls `executeWithdrawal(Alice, E)` to process her pending request.\n4.  The call to `Math.mulDiv` inside `executeWithdrawal` reverts with a panic code `0x12` (division by zero).\n5.  Alice's withdrawal is now stuck. She cannot successfully call `executeWithdrawal` for epoch `E`.\n6.  She also cannot call `cancelWithdrawal`, as it would only apply to a request in the current epoch, `E+1`.\n7.  **Outcome:** Alice's funds associated with the withdrawal request in epoch `E` are permanently locked and cannot be retrieved via the standard contract functions.\n\n### Recommendations\n\nThe vulnerability can be fixed by adding a zero-check in `executeWithdrawal` before the `Math.mulDiv` call. This handles the edge case gracefully by recognizing that if zero shares were fulfilled, zero assets should be disbursed, and allows the remaining logic (which moves the request to the next epoch) to proceed without reverting.\n\n```solidity\n// src/HypoVault.sol\n\n        // assets to withdraw = prorated shares to withdraw * assets fulfilled / total shares fulfilled\n        uint256 assetsToWithdraw;\n        if (_withdrawalEpochState.sharesFulfilled == 0) {\n            assetsToWithdraw = 0;\n        } else {\n            assetsToWithdraw = Math.mulDiv(\n                sharesToFulfill,\n                _withdrawalEpochState.assetsReceived,\n                _withdrawalEpochState.sharesFulfilled\n            );\n        }\n\n        reservedWithdrawalAssets -= assetsToWithdraw;\n```\n\nThis change should replace the original single-line `assetsToWithdraw` calculation.\n\nWrite a detailed description of the root cause and impact(s) of this finding.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nDescribe the best method(s) to mitigate the finding.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"./HypoVault.t.sol\";\nimport \"./PanopticVaultAccountant.t.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is Test {\n    // Vault Data\n    VaultAccountantMock public mockAccountant;\n    HypoVault public vault;\n    ERC20S public token;\n\n    // Accountant Data\n    PanopticVaultAccountant public accountant;\n    MockERC20Partial public token0;\n    MockERC20Partial public token1;\n    MockERC20Partial public underlyingToken;\n    MockV3CompatibleOracle public poolOracle;\n    MockV3CompatibleOracle public oracle0;\n    MockV3CompatibleOracle public oracle1;\n    MockPanopticPool public mockPool;\n\n    // Test Addresses\n    address Manager = address(0x1234);\n    address FeeWallet = address(0x5678);\n    address Alice = address(0x123456);\n    address Bob = address(0x12345678);\n    address Charlie = address(0x1234567891);\n    address Dave = address(0x12345678912);\n    address Eve = address(0x123456789123);\n\n    // Test Parameters\n    uint256 constant INITIAL_BALANCE = 1000000 ether;\n    uint256 constant BOOTSTRAP_SHARES = 1_000_000;\n    int24 constant TWAP_TICK = 100;\n    int24 constant MAX_PRICE_DEVIATION = 50;\n    uint32 constant TWAP_WINDOW = 600; // 10 minutes\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        mockAccountant = new VaultAccountantMock();\n        token = new ERC20S(\"Test Token\", \"TEST\", 18);\n        vault = new HypoVault(address(token), Manager, IVaultAccountant(address(mockAccountant)), 100); // 1% performance fee\n        mockAccountant.setExpectedVault(address(vault));\n\n        // Set fee wallet\n        vault.setFeeWallet(FeeWallet);\n\n        // Mint tokens and approve vault for all users\n        address[6] memory users = [Alice, Bob, Charlie, Dave, Eve, Manager];\n        for (uint i = 0; i < users.length; i++) {\n            token.mint(users[i], INITIAL_BALANCE);\n            vm.prank(users[i]);\n            token.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);\n        }\n        \n        accountant = new PanopticVaultAccountant();\n        token0 = new MockERC20Partial(\"Token0\", \"T0\");\n        token1 = new MockERC20Partial(\"Token1\", \"T1\");\n        underlyingToken = new MockERC20Partial(\"Underlying\", \"UND\");\n        poolOracle = new MockV3CompatibleOracle();\n        oracle0 = new MockV3CompatibleOracle();\n        oracle1 = new MockV3CompatibleOracle();\n        mockPool = new MockPanopticPool();\n\n        // Setup default oracle behavior\n        _setupDefaultOracles();\n    }\n\n    function _setupDefaultOracles() internal {\n        // Setup consistent tick cumulatives for TWAP calculation\n        // The TWAP filter computes (tickCumulative[i] - tickCumulative[i+1]) / (twapWindow / 20)\n        // So for a constant tick of 100, we need cumulative differences of 100 * (twapWindow / 20)\n        int56[] memory defaultTicks = new int56[](20);\n        uint32 intervalDuration = TWAP_WINDOW / 20; // 30 seconds\n\n        // Create tick cumulatives that will result in TWAP_TICK when filtered\n        for (uint i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\n            defaultTicks[i] = int56(\n                int256(TWAP_TICK) * int256(uint256(intervalDuration)) * int256(20 - i)\n            );\n        }\n        poolOracle.setTickCumulatives(defaultTicks);\n        oracle0.setTickCumulatives(defaultTicks);\n        oracle1.setTickCumulatives(defaultTicks);\n    }\n\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // Proof of Concept code demonstrating the vulnerability\n\n        // --- 1. SETUP ---\n        // Alice deposits 100 tokens to get shares in the vault.\n        uint128 aliceDepositAmount = 100 * 10**18;\n        vm.startPrank(Alice);\n        vault.requestDeposit(aliceDepositAmount);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Manager fulfills the deposit so Alice receives shares.\n        // We get the current deposit epoch before it's incremented.\n        uint256 depositEpoch = vault.depositEpoch();\n        vm.startPrank(Manager);\n        // We simulate the NAV update. The mock accountant simply returns the value we set.\n        // Total assets are now the initial supply (from bootstrapping) plus Alice's deposit.\n        mockAccountant.setNav(vault.totalSupply() + aliceDepositAmount);\n        vault.fulfillDeposits(aliceDepositAmount, \"\");\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Alice executes the deposit to finalize receiving her shares.\n        vm.startPrank(Alice);\n        vault.executeDeposit(Alice, depositEpoch);\n        uint256 aliceShares = vault.balanceOf(Alice);\n        assertTrue(aliceShares > 0, \"Alice should have received shares\");\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // --- 2. TRIGGER VULNERABILITY ---\n        // Alice requests to withdraw all her shares.\n        uint128 sharesToWithdraw = uint128(aliceShares);\n        uint256 withdrawalEpoch = vault.withdrawalEpoch(); // This is epoch 0\n        vm.startPrank(Alice);\n        vault.requestWithdrawal(sharesToWithdraw);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // The Manager maliciously or accidentally calls fulfillWithdrawals with sharesToFulfill = 0.\n        // This is the core of the vulnerability.\n        vm.startPrank(Manager);\n        mockAccountant.setNav(vault.totalSupply()); // NAV is based on current total supply\n        vault.fulfillWithdrawals(0, type(uint256).max, \"\");\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // --- 3. VERIFY LOCKED STATE ---\n        // The withdrawal epoch has advanced, but the previous epoch (0) is now broken.\n        assertEq(vault.withdrawalEpoch(), withdrawalEpoch + 1, \"Withdrawal epoch should have advanced\");\n        (,, uint128 sharesFulfilled) = vault.withdrawalEpochState(withdrawalEpoch);\n        assertEq(sharesFulfilled, 0, \"sharesFulfilled for the epoch is 0\");\n\n        // Alice tries to execute her withdrawal. The transaction reverts due to division by zero.\n        vm.startPrank(Alice);\n        // We expect a revert. In Solidity >=0.8.0, division by zero results in a Panic(0x12).\n        // A bare vm.expectRevert() will catch any revert, including panics.\n        vm.expectRevert();\n        vault.executeWithdrawal(Alice, withdrawalEpoch);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        // --- 4. VERIFY CANCELLATION IS NOT POSSIBLE ---\n        // The manager trying to cancel will operate on the *current* epoch (1), not the stuck one (0).\n        vm.prank(Manager);\n        vault.cancelWithdrawal(Alice); // This does not revert, but it doesn't help.\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Alice's withdrawal request for epoch 0 remains stuck forever.\n        (uint128 amount, ) = vault.queuedWithdrawal(Alice, withdrawalEpoch);\n        assertEq(amount, sharesToWithdraw, \"Withdrawal request is still queued and locked\");\n    }\n}\n\n```",
